@*
    The roadmap: the planning view, taking the whole screen below the header as a
    surface of its own. It used to be a band capped at three tenths of the screen
    above the task panes, and a plan read across time in a strip that short was a
    plan paged through a few rows at a time; the panes beside it were never what the
    reader was planning with. So the shell shows it instead of the panes, the way it
    shows Tools and the Dashboard.

    It draws the stored plan (.domain/roadmap) through the shared timeline
    control: a band per configured repository, the person's own lanes inside each,
    spans and milestones placed by their dates, and an arrow from each thing that
    has to land first to the thing waiting on it. A plan filed in several
    repositories is drawn in each of their bands, as that repository's part. The
    chart shows plans and not their tasks: a bar carries its progress fill, and the
    tasks themselves stay in the task list. The chart owns its own scrollbar, so
    the heading and the filters above it stay put.

    What the band never does is edit the plan it was handed. RoadmapTimeline
    proposes a placement when something is dragged or moved with the keyboard;
    this asks the module whether that placement stands, and redraws from what
    comes back. Only the plan knows whether a window is valid and what else the
    change means.

    A plan with nothing in it still gets an empty state rather than an empty
    chart.

    Whether the roadmap is on screen is decided in the shell's header, in the
    surface switcher beside Workspace, Tools and Dashboard. So there is no
    affordance on the view itself and no visibility state of its own to hold.

    Beside the chart, or under it on a narrow window, sits the unplanned work: the
    imported plans that have no item yet, whose tasks arrived first (ADR 0013,
    ruling 3), one card each with its share per repository. Picking one runs the
    same import a roadmap document runs, so the item lands placed and sized
    exactly as an imported one does. With nothing to offer the column is not drawn
    and the chart takes the width.
*@
@implements IDisposable
@inject IRoadmapPlanning Planning
@inject IRoadmapItemRollup Rollups
@inject IImportedPlanSource ImportedPlans
@inject GitHubSettingsStore Repositories
@inject IRoadmapWorkChanges WorkChanges

<section class="roadmap-band" id="roadmap-band" aria-labelledby="roadmap-band-title" data-testid="roadmap-band">
    @* The heading lives in one of two places, and which one depends on whether
       there is a chart to head.

       With a plan, it is handed to RoadmapTimeline as its own heading. The chart's
       header is already a row with a heading at one end and the filter controls at
       the other, so giving it this one puts "Roadmap" and the filters on the same
       line, and a chart heading of its own would have been a second title saying
       the same thing.

       With nothing planned there is no chart, so the view carries its own header
       above the empty state. Either way the h2 keeps the id this section's
       aria-labelledby names. *@
    <div class="@BodyClass" data-testid="roadmap-band-body">
        <div class="roadmap-band__content" data-testid="roadmap-band-content">
            @if (_view.HasAnythingToDraw)
            {
                @* ShowSteps off: the roadmap reads plans, and a bar opening into a row
                   per task put the whole backlog back on a chart meant to show what is
                   planned when. The fill still says how far along each part is. *@
                <RoadmapTimeline Groups="@_view.Groups"
                                 Bars="@_view.Bars"
                                 Milestones="@_view.Milestones"
                                 Links="@_view.Links"
                                 Today="@Today"
                                 TitleContent="@BandHeading"
                                 LabelledBy="roadmap-band-title"
                                 ShowSteps="false"
                                 SelectedId="@_selectedId"
                                 OnBarSelected="Select"
                                 OnBarChanged="Apply"
                                 OnLinkCreated="LinkAsync"
                                 CssClass="roadmap-band__timeline"
                                 TestId="roadmap-timeline" />

                @* Refusals are shown, not swallowed. The library's alert renders nothing
                   when there is no message, so there is no empty element in the band on
                   the ordinary path — and role="status" rather than "alert" because the
                   chart has already redrawn the bar back to where it was, so this is the
                   explanation rather than the news. *@
                <Alert Message="@_error" Role="status" TestId="roadmap-band-error" />
            }
            else
            {
                @* Nothing planned, so the only affordance is the one that starts a plan.
                   It sits in the band's own header here, because there is no chart header
                   for it to live in. *@
                <header class="roadmap-band__header">
                    @BandHeading
                </header>

                <EmptyState TestId="roadmap-band-empty-state"
                            CssClass="roadmap-band__empty"
                            Title="No roadmap yet"
                            Description="Planned work appears here on a timeline, in a band per configured repository. Priorities and dependencies are the plan's own; status is read from the backlog entry a plan item is linked to." />
            }
        </div>

        <RoadmapPlanShelf Plans="@_unplanned"
                          Busy="@_planning"
                          OnPlan="PlanFromShelfAsync"
                          CssClass="roadmap-band__shelf"
                          TestId="roadmap-shelf" />
    </div>

    @* Outside the shelf, because planning its last row takes the shelf away and a
       live region inside it would go with it. What was planned is only heard — the
       bar appearing on the chart is what a sighted reader sees — and a refusal is
       shown as well, since the row it concerns is still there. *@
    <Alert Message="@_shelfNews"
           Role="status"
           CssClass="@(_shelfRefused ? "roadmap-band__shelf-news" : "sr-only")"
           TestId="roadmap-shelf-news" />

    <RoadmapItemEditor @ref="_editor"
                       Open="@_editing"
                       OpenChanged="EditingChanged"
                       Item="@_edited"
                       Rollup="@_rollup"
                       Repositories="@Configured()"
                       DependencyOptions="@DependencyOptions()"
                       Today="@(Today ?? DateOnly.FromDateTime(DateTime.Today))"
                       OnSave="SaveAsync"
                       OnDelete="DeleteAsync"
                       OnDependencyChanged="ChangeDependencyAsync" />

    <RoadmapMilestoneEditor @ref="_dateEditor"
                            Open="@_editingDate"
                            OpenChanged="EditingDateChanged"
                            Milestone="@_editedDate"
                            Repositories="@Configured()"
                            DependencyOptions="@DependencyOptions()"
                            Today="@(Today ?? DateOnly.FromDateTime(DateTime.Today))"
                            OnSave="SaveDateAsync"
                            OnDelete="DeleteDateAsync"
                            OnDependencyChanged="ChangeDependencyAsync" />
</section>

@code {
    // The plan as the timeline needs it. Rebuilt from the module after every
    // change rather than patched in place: the plan decides what a reschedule did,
    // and a locally applied guess is one more thing that can disagree with it.
    private RoadmapTimelineModel _view = RoadmapTimelineModel.Empty;

    // The plan itself as well as the view of it. The editor needs the items — their
    // titles, their dependencies — and a bar on the chart carries only what it takes
    // to draw one.
    private RoadmapPlanDto _plan = RoadmapPlanDto.Empty;

    private RoadmapItemEditor? _editor;
    private RoadmapItemDto? _edited;
    private RoadmapItemRollupDto _rollup = RoadmapItemRollupDto.Empty;
    private bool _editing;

    private RoadmapMilestoneEditor? _dateEditor;
    private RoadmapMilestoneDto? _editedDate;
    private bool _editingDate;

    private string? _selectedId;
    private string? _error;

    // Imported plans no item carries the tag of, as the shelf offers them, and the
    // reading they were built from — which is what a pressed row is planned with.
    private IReadOnlyList<RoadmapShelfPlan> _unplanned = [];
    private IReadOnlyList<ImportedPlanDto> _imported = [];
    private string? _planning;
    private string? _shelfNews;
    private bool _shelfRefused;

    /// <summary>The chart and, while there is unplanned work, the column beside it.
    /// Without any the chart takes the width rather than leaving a gutter.</summary>
    private string BodyClass => _unplanned.Count > 0
        ? "roadmap-band__body roadmap-band__body--with-shelf"
        : "roadmap-band__body";

    /// <summary>
    /// The band's heading, in one place because it is rendered in two.
    /// <para>
    /// The years the plan covers used to be the chart's title, on the reasoning that
    /// the band already said "Roadmap" and a second copy of the word would read as a
    /// different thing. Both headings are now one heading, so the year has nowhere to
    /// go that is worth a line — the quarters are ruled across the axis with their
    /// years on them, which is where a reader looks for a date anyway.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment BandHeading => @<text>
        <div class="roadmap-band__title-lines">
            <p class="roadmap-band__eyebrow">Planning</p>
            <h2 id="roadmap-band-title" class="roadmap-band__title">Roadmap</h2>
        </div>

        @* Beside the heading rather than out at the end with the filters: these add to
           the plan, and the filters only change what is shown of it. *@
        <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                   Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                   OnClick="PlanSomething"
                   TestId="roadmap-band-add">
            Plan work
        </AppButton>

        <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                   Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                   OnClick="AddADate"
                   TestId="roadmap-band-add-date">
            Add a date
        </AppButton>

        @* After the controls that add to the plan: it decides how long the next
           imported plan is drawn, not what is on the chart now. *@
        <RoadmapPaceControl />
    </text>;

    /// <summary>Where the "now" line is drawn. A parameter so a test can put
    /// today somewhere it chooses rather than wherever the clock happens to
    /// be.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public DateOnly? Today { get; set; }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        Repositories.Changed += OnRepositoriesChanged;
        Planning.Changed += OnPlanChanged;
        WorkChanges.Changed += OnWorkChanged;
        await Load();
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Repositories.Changed -= OnRepositoriesChanged;
        Planning.Changed -= OnPlanChanged;
        WorkChanges.Changed -= OnWorkChanged;
    }

    private async Task Load()
    {
        _plan = await Planning.GetPlanAsync();
        CarryOverPlanColours();

        // Every item's gathered work in one read, not one per bar: the steps drawn
        // inside each bar, and its progress fill, come from here (ADR 0013, ruling
        // 6). Read on every load and never kept — progress belongs to Tasks.
        var rollups = await Rollups.GatherPlanAsync(_plan);

        _view = RoadmapPlanView.From(_plan, Configured(), rollups);

        _imported = await ImportedPlans.ListAsync();
        _unplanned = Unplanned(_imported, _plan);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The imported plans no item on the plan carries the tag of, in the order the
    /// backlog holds them.
    /// <para>
    /// Compared as slugs, the way the module stores an item's tag, so a plan tag
    /// written with a capital is still recognised as planned once its item exists.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    internal static IReadOnlyList<RoadmapShelfPlan> Unplanned(
        IReadOnlyList<ImportedPlanDto> imported,
        RoadmapPlanDto plan)
    {
        var planned = plan.Items
            .Where(item => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(item.Tag))
            .Select(item => RoadmapTagSlug.From(item.Tag))
            .ToHashSet(StringComparer.Ordinal);

        return
        [
            .. imported
                .Where(candidate => !planned.Contains(RoadmapTagSlug.From(candidate.Tag)))
                .Select(candidate => new RoadmapShelfPlan(
                    candidate.Tag,
                    candidate.RepositoryAliases,
                    candidate.TaskCount,
                    candidate.TotalEffort,
                    candidate.UnestimatedCount,
                    // Only for a plan spanning repositories: one repository's share
                    // of a one-repository plan is the plan, said twice.
                    candidate.Parts.Count > 1
                        ? [.. candidate.Parts.Select(part => new RoadmapShelfPart(
                            part.RepositoryAlias,
                            part.TaskCount,
                            part.TotalEffort,
                            part.UnestimatedCount))]
                        : null))
        ];
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Plans one shelf row: a single plan-level entry built from it, run through the
    /// same import a roadmap document goes through, with the effort its tasks already
    /// registered — so the window is the one an import would have drawn.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task PlanFromShelfAsync(RoadmapShelfPlan row)
    {
        if (_planning is not null) return;

        var imported = _imported.FirstOrDefault(candidate =>
            string.Equals(candidate.Tag, row.Tag, StringComparison.Ordinal));
        if (imported is null) return;

        _planning = row.Tag;
        _shelfNews = null;
        _shelfRefused = false;

        try
        {
            var entry = new PlanImportEntryDto(
                Title: TitleOf(imported.Tag),
                Tag: imported.Tag,
                RepositoryAliases: imported.RepositoryAliases);

            var result = await Planning.ImportPlanItemsAsync([entry], [imported.Effort]);

            if (result.IsFailure)
            {
                _shelfRefused = true;
                _shelfNews = $"+{row.Tag} could not be planned: {result.Error.Message}";
                return;
            }

            _shelfNews = $"Planned +{row.Tag} on the roadmap.";
            await Load();
        }
        finally
        {
            _planning = null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The item's title, read off its tag — the only name a tasks-first plan
    /// has. <c>release-q4</c> becomes <c>Release q4</c>; the editor is where a person
    /// gives it a better one.</summary>
    internal static string TitleOf(string tag)
    {
        var words = tag.Replace('-', ' ').Replace('_', ' ').Trim();
        return words.Length == 0
            ? tag
            : char.ToUpper(words[0], CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) + words[1..];
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Moves colours chosen before the choice lived in Settings onto the repositories
    /// they were chosen for.
    /// <para>
    /// A plan written by an older version stores a hue per alias. That choice was a real
    /// decision somebody made and losing it would be the upgrade quietly recolouring
    /// their chart, so it is carried over the first time the roadmap is opened. Only
    /// onto a repository that has no colour of its own — a choice made in Settings since
    /// is the newer answer and is never overwritten by the file.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Here rather than at startup because this is the only screen that reads a plan,
    /// and a startup path that loaded one just to look for colours would make every
    /// launch pay for a migration that concerns one screen.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void CarryOverPlanColours()
    {
        if (_plan.Bands.Count == 0) return;

        foreach (var repository in Repositories.Current.Repositories.Where(r => r.Colour is null))
        {
            if (!_plan.Bands.TryGetValue(repository.Alias, out var colour)) continue;

            Repositories.SetRepositoryColour(repository.Alias, colour);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The configured repositories, in the order Settings lists them. The
    /// band asks per load rather than caching: a repository added while the app is
    /// open should show up when the plan is next read, not after a restart.</summary>
    /// <summary>
    /// The configured repositories as the band needs them, each carrying the identity
    /// hue Settings resolved for it. The roadmap is told which hue rather than choosing
    /// one, so the same project is the same colour here, on the repository filter and
    /// on an entry row.
    /// <para>
    /// The visible hues rather than the chosen ones, so a workspace with the
    /// visualization off draws its bands the way this chart has always drawn a group
    /// nobody gave a hue to: no colour at all. Note that
    /// <see cref="CarryOverPlanColours"/> above deliberately reads the raw choice — a
    /// migration that only ran while the hues happened to be on screen would lose
    /// somebody's old plan colours for good.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private List<PlannedRepository> Configured()
    {
        var colours = Repositories.Current.VisibleColours();

        return
        [
            .. Repositories.Current.Repositories.Select(repository =>
                new PlannedRepository(
                    repository.Alias,
                    repository.FullName,
                    colours.TryGetValue(repository.Alias, out var colour) ? colour : null))
        ];
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A bar was opened — clicked, or Enter pressed on it, which the chart's own
    /// keyboard help calls "open". So it opens the editor for that item.
    /// <para>
    /// A milestone is selectable too and has no editor yet, so opening one selects it
    /// and stops there rather than showing a dialog with the wrong fields in it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Select(string barId)
    {
        _selectedId = barId;

        var id = RoadmapPlanView.NodeIdOf(barId);
        if (id is null) return;

        var item = _plan.Items.FirstOrDefault(candidate => candidate.Id == id);
        if (item is not null)
        {
            // Gathered before the dialog opens, so the item's linked and tagged work is
            // on screen with it rather than a render behind it. The band asks its own
            // module port; the adapter behind it is the one thing allowed to read both
            // the backlog and the knowledge graph.
            _rollup = await Rollups.GatherAsync(item);
            _edited = item;
            _editing = true;
            return;
        }

        var milestone = _plan.Milestones.FirstOrDefault(candidate => candidate.Id == id);
        if (milestone is null) return;

        _editedDate = milestone;
        _editingDate = true;
    }

    private void PlanSomething()
    {
        // Nothing to gather for work that does not exist yet.
        _rollup = RoadmapItemRollupDto.Empty;
        _edited = null;
        _editing = true;
    }

    private void AddADate()
    {
        _editedDate = null;
        _editingDate = true;
    }

    private void EditingDateChanged(bool open)
    {
        _editingDate = open;
        if (!open) _editedDate = null;
    }

    private void EditingChanged(bool open)
    {
        _editing = open;
        if (!open) _edited = null;
    }

    /// <summary>Everything in the plan an item could be told to wait for, itself
    /// excluded — the plan would refuse that, but offering it at all is the kind of
    /// choice a control should not present.</summary>
    private List<SelectorOption> DependencyOptions() =>
    [
        .. _plan.Items
            .Where(item => item.Id != _edited?.Id)
            .Select(item => new SelectorOption(item.Id.ToString(), item.Title)),
        .. _plan.Milestones
            .Where(milestone => milestone.Id != _edited?.Id)
            .Select(milestone => new SelectorOption(milestone.Id.ToString(), milestone.Title))
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// Add or edit, decided by whether the submission carries an id.
    /// <para>
    /// The dialog closes only when the module accepted it. A refusal is shown inside
    /// the dialog, on the fields that caused it, rather than behind a dialog that
    /// closed as though the save had worked.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task SaveAsync(RoadmapEditorSubmission submission)
    {
        _error = null;

        var saved = submission.ItemId is null
            ? await Planning.AddItemAsync(
                submission.Title,
                submission.Start,
                submission.End,
                submission.Priority,
                submission.RepositoryAliases,
                submission.Lane,
                submission.TaskId,
                submission.Notes,
                submission.Tag,
                submission.KnowledgeRefs)
            : await Planning.UpdateItemAsync(
                submission.ItemId.Value,
                submission.Title,
                submission.Start,
                submission.End,
                submission.Priority,
                submission.RepositoryAliases,
                submission.Lane,
                submission.TaskId,
                submission.Notes,
                submission.Tag,
                submission.KnowledgeRefs);

        if (saved.IsFailure)
        {
            _editor?.Refuse(saved.Error.Message);
            return;
        }

        _editing = false;
        _edited = null;
        await Load();
    }

    private async Task DeleteAsync(Guid itemId)
    {
        _error = null;

        var removed = await Planning.RemoveItemAsync(itemId);

        if (removed.IsFailure)
        {
            _editor?.Refuse(removed.Error.Message);
            return;
        }

        _editing = false;
        _edited = null;
        _selectedId = null;
        await Load();
    }

    private async Task SaveDateAsync(RoadmapMilestoneSubmission submission)
    {
        _error = null;

        var saved = submission.MilestoneId is null
            ? await Planning.AddMilestoneAsync(
                submission.Title,
                submission.On,
                submission.Kind,
                submission.RepositoryAliases,
                null,
                submission.IsPlanWide)
            : await Planning.UpdateMilestoneAsync(
                submission.MilestoneId.Value,
                submission.Title,
                submission.On,
                submission.Kind,
                submission.RepositoryAliases,
                null,
                submission.IsPlanWide);

        if (saved.IsFailure)
        {
            _dateEditor?.Refuse(saved.Error.Message);
            return;
        }

        _editingDate = false;
        _editedDate = null;
        await Load();
    }

    private async Task DeleteDateAsync(Guid milestoneId)
    {
        _error = null;

        var removed = await Planning.RemoveMilestoneAsync(milestoneId);

        if (removed.IsFailure)
        {
            _dateEditor?.Refuse(removed.Error.Message);
            return;
        }

        _editingDate = false;
        _editedDate = null;
        _selectedId = null;
        await Load();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// One dependency, added or taken away, answered while the dialog is still open.
    /// <para>
    /// A refused edge — one that would close a cycle — is reported in the dialog and
    /// the plan is reloaded, so the tick the person just made goes back to where the
    /// stored plan says it should be.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ChangeDependencyAsync(RoadmapDependencyChange change)
    {
        var result = change.Added
            ? await Planning.AddDependencyAsync(change.NodeId, change.DependsOnId)
            : await Planning.RemoveDependencyAsync(change.NodeId, change.DependsOnId);

        await Load();

        if (result.IsFailure)
        {
            // Whichever dialog raised it: a milestone waits on things just as an item
            // does, and the refusal has to land where the person is looking.
            if (_editingDate) _dateEditor?.Refuse(result.Error.Message);
            else _editor?.Refuse(result.Error.Message);

            return;
        }

        // Re-point the dialog at the reloaded node, so what it shows and what is
        // stored cannot disagree about which dependencies exist.
        if (_edited is not null)
        {
            _edited = _plan.Items.FirstOrDefault(item => item.Id == _edited.Id) ?? _edited;
        }

        if (_editedDate is not null)
        {
            _editedDate = _plan.Milestones.FirstOrDefault(date => date.Id == _editedDate.Id) ?? _editedDate;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A drag or a keyboard move, settled. The timeline has already snapped the
    /// dates to the week and told us where the bar landed; the plan decides whether
    /// that stands.
    /// <para>
    /// The lane only travels when the bar actually changed row. Passing the current
    /// lane on every move would refile work that merely moved in time, and passing
    /// null on a row change would drop the one thing the gesture was about.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task Apply(RoadmapChange change)
    {
        var itemId = RoadmapPlanView.NodeIdOf(change.BarId);
        if (itemId is null) return;

        _error = null;

        var (start, end) = ItemWindowFor(change, itemId.Value);

        var rescheduled = await Planning.RescheduleItemAsync(
            itemId.Value,
            start,
            end,
            LaneOf(change.RowId));

        // Reload either way. On success the plan is the truth; on failure the
        // redraw is what puts the bar back where it was, so a refused change never
        // leaves the chart showing something that was not stored.
        if (rescheduled.IsFailure) _error = rescheduled.Error.Message;

        await Load();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The item's own window, from where one of its bars was put.
    /// <para>
    /// A bar may be one segment of an item whose tasks hand over between
    /// repositories, and then its dates are a slice of the item's window rather than
    /// the window itself. So what is carried over is how far each edge moved: dragging
    /// a segment a week moves the whole item a week, and pulling a segment's end out
    /// lengthens the item by as much. For a bar that is the whole item, the two readings
    /// are the same.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private (DateOnly Start, DateOnly End) ItemWindowFor(RoadmapChange change, Guid itemId)
    {
        var bar = _view.Bars.FirstOrDefault(candidate => candidate.Id == change.BarId);
        var item = _plan.Items.FirstOrDefault(candidate => candidate.Id == itemId);

        return bar is null || item is null
            ? (change.Start, change.End)
            : RoadmapPlanView.ItemWindowFor(item, bar, change);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A dependency drawn on the chart: the handle past one bar's end dropped on
    /// another, which is to wait for it. Answered the way the editor's checkbox is —
    /// the plan decides, a refusal such as a cycle is shown, and the arrow appears
    /// from the reloaded plan rather than from the gesture.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task LinkAsync(RoadmapLink link)
    {
        var dependsOn = RoadmapPlanView.NodeIdOf(link.FromId);
        var waiting = RoadmapPlanView.NodeIdOf(link.ToId);
        if (dependsOn is null || waiting is null) return;

        _error = dependsOn == waiting
            ? "A plan cannot wait for another part of itself."
            : null;

        if (_error is null)
        {
            var added = await Planning.AddDependencyAsync(waiting.Value, dependsOn.Value);
            if (added.IsFailure) _error = added.Error.Message;
        }

        await Load();
    }

    /// <summary>The lane out of a row id, as the view built it. A stacked row of a
    /// lane names that lane too, so a bar dropped onto one stays filed where it
    /// was.</summary>
    private static string? LaneOf(string rowId) => RoadmapPlanView.LaneOf(rowId);

    private void OnRepositoriesChanged() => InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        await Load();
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    /// <summary>
    /// The plan changed somewhere else — Import in the Tasks pane, an MCP tool — while
    /// the band was on screen, so it is read again rather than left to wait for the
    /// band to be toggled off and on.
    /// <para>
    /// The band's own writes raise this too, after it has already reloaded for them.
    /// The second read is the same plan and costs one query; telling our own changes
    /// apart from everyone else's would be a second bookkeeping of the plan.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void OnPlanChanged() => ReloadForOutsideChange();

    /// <summary>A gathered task was written — a step marked done, a task-level import —
    /// so the steps, the fill and the shelf are read again from what Tasks now
    /// holds.</summary>
    private void OnWorkChanged() => ReloadForOutsideChange();

    /// <summary>A reload is already under way when the next change lands.</summary>
    private bool _reloading;

    /// <summary>One more change arrived while it was.</summary>
    private bool _reloadAgain;

    /// <summary>
    /// Reads the band again after a change made outside it.
    /// <para>
    /// Coalesced, because an import writes every entry it brings in one after another
    /// and each write is heard: the changes that land during a reload are answered
    /// by one more reload after it, not by one each.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void ReloadForOutsideChange() => InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        if (_reloading)
        {
            _reloadAgain = true;
            return;
        }

        _reloading = true;
        try
        {
            do
            {
                _reloadAgain = false;
                await Load();
                StateHasChanged();
            }
            while (_reloadAgain);
        }
        finally
        {
            _reloading = false;
        }
    });
}
